TRACING AND DEBUGGING SYSTEM ACTIVITY

Available syntax (from trace.c):
  trace on             enable default tracing (SVC focus)
  trace on -s          record syscall events only
  trace on -a          record all supported contexts/events
  trace off            stop recording
  trace show [PID]     print stored records; STOPS recording
  trace follow [PID]   continuous interactive monitor
  trace run [-s|-a] command [args...]

Use trace show/trace on from MCP. Avoid trace follow (never exits
under normal use). trace run starts a child and may hit MCP's
3-second timeout, limited process slots and 4096-byte output cap.
Trace buffers are finite; events may be overwritten under load.

Recommended bounded MCP investigation:
  ps
  trace on -s
  cat /proc/net/memory
  trace show
  trace on
If needed capture a short command while tracing. 'trace show'
turns recording off, even if it was enabled previously. Re-enable.

Event interpretation:
  SYSCALL entry/exit identifies number, FD, target, result.
  SWITCH/TIMER/KERNEL contexts appear in -a mode, if compiled.
  A negative return indicates failure in many syscall wrappers.
  For fd paths, open/close can alter a descriptor's meaning;
  trace uses a small path-name cache, not a full audit trail.
  Symbol addresses in /proc/sym/kernel can help correlate PCs.

Trace itself changes timing: a high-volume trace may alter
scheduling, memory use, and UART/network output. Prefer -s first.
Use /man/memory for symbol mapping and bounded hexdump.
If output gets large, filter trace show PID or reduce workload.
